\section{Discussion of Problems}

9.1\quad \textit{Long-Term Fluence Monitoring:}

9.1.1\quad As indicated in the cobalt method, low-concentration cobalt-aluminum alloy
wire is suitable for monitoring long-term thermal fluence. When the thermal neutron
fluence rate being monitored exceeds $10^{14}$~cm$^{-2}$$\cdot$s$^{-1}$ and the exposure
times exceed a few weeks, the experimenter must be aware of possible burn-up of the
target $^{59}$Co nuclei and of the $^{60}$Co being formed in the monitor, and burnout of
the $^{113}$Cd, if a cadmium sleeve is being used. A correction of the burnup of
$^{59}$Co and $^{60}$Co can be made by solving the following equation~(6) for $\varphi$:

\begin{equation}
  A = \frac{N_0\,\sigma_1\,\varphi\,\lambda
    \!\left[\exp\!\left(-(\varphi\sigma_1 t_i)\right)
    - \exp\!\left(-(\varphi\sigma_2+\lambda)t_i\right)\right]}
  {\left[\varphi(\sigma_2-\sigma_1)+\lambda\right]\exp(\lambda t_w)}
  \label{eq:burnup}
\end{equation}

\noindent where:
\begin{align*}
  A       &= \text{measured }^{60}\text{Co activity,}\\
  N_0     &= \text{original number of }^{59}\text{Co atoms (see 8.1.3),}\\
  \sigma_1 &= {}^{59}\text{Co cross section} = 37.233\ \text{barns,}\\
  \sigma_2 &= {}^{60}\text{Co cross section} = 2\ \text{barns,}\\
  \lambda &= {}^{60}\text{Co decay constant} = 4.16647\times10^{-9},\\
  t_i     &= \text{exposure time, s, and}\\
  t_w     &= \text{elapsed time after the end of the exposure.}
\end{align*}

9.1.2\quad The method of dealing with long-term irradiations in which variations in
fluence rate occur is discussed in Practice E261.

9.2\quad \textit{High-Temperature Measurements:}

9.2.1\quad As stated in the scope of this method, the methods being reported are
essentially limited to room temperature environments. At higher temperatures, two basic
problems arise. First, the most probable velocity for the Maxwell-Boltzmann distribution
of neutrons in thermal equilibrium with their moderator shifts upward from the 2200~m/s
value for 20~$^\circ$C. In the interpretation of fluence rate, the experimenter must
make the correct choice of cross sections, be aware of the departure of the cross
sections from the 1/v-law, and remember that the effective cadmium cut-off energy is a
function of temperature for a given thickness of cadmium. Second, pure cadmium has a
relatively low melting point (321~$^\circ$C) and gives considerable trouble for
temperatures above 100~$^\circ$C. It has been observed that above 100~$^\circ$C, cadmium
metal tends to diffuse rapidly into metals in contact with it. Thus, standard gold or
indium foils are immediately ruined when cadmium covered at these temperatures by
receiving a readily observed layer of cadmium impregnated on their surfaces. Two possible
compounds of cadmium for high-temperature experiments are cadmium oxide and cadmium
silicate. The melting points for these compounds are above 900~$^\circ$C. Equivalent
cadmium metal thicknesses, stability under intense gamma and neutron bombardment, and
fabrication properties would have to be determined before these compounds could be
reliably used. Gadolinium filters, which have a lower cut-off energy than cadmium, have
been successfully used~(14). For high temperatures, Co-Ni or Co-V alloy detectors should
be selected instead of the Co-Al alloy referred to in 9.1.1.

9.2.2\quad An alternate method for eliminating the use of cadmium at elevated temperatures
is presented in Practice E481. The method uses one monitor (cobalt) with nearly a 1/v
absorption cross section curve and a second monitor (silver) with a large resonance peak
so that its resonance integral is large compared to the thermal cross section. The method
relies on the assumption that the epithermal part of the spectrum follows a 1/$E$
distribution. In this method, the activities of both cobalt and silver monitors are
determined by the radiometric technique. This differs from the method described in 8.1
wherein only the activity of cobalt is determined radiometrically. The advantages of
Practice E481 are the elimination of three difficulties associated with the use of
cadmium: (1) the perturbation of the neutron field by the cadmium, (2) the inexact
cadmium cut-off energy, and (3) the low melting temperature of cadmium. Studies indicate
that the accuracy of the two-reaction method can be comparable to the cadmium-ratio
method. Also, the long half-lives of the two monitors, cobalt and silver, permit the
determination of fluence for long-term monitoring.

